# What Is a Database? Data, Tables, and the DBMS

> Every application you use — banking, shopping, attendance portals —
> rests on a database that stores, protects, and retrieves its data.
> This lesson defines the database, shows why the DBMS replaced plain
> files, and introduces tables, keys, and data independence.

## 1. Data, Information, and the Storage Problem

[definition]
A **database** is an organised, persistent collection of interrelated
data stored so that it can be searched, updated, and shared reliably.
A **DBMS** (Database Management System) is the software that stands
between users and that data: MySQL, PostgreSQL, Oracle, SQL Server,
SQLite, and MongoDB are all DBMSs.
[/definition]

Raw *data* are bare facts — 91, "Aisha", 55000. *Information* is data
placed in context so it answers a question: "Aisha scored 91, the
highest in the batch." Databases exist so that turning data into
information is one cheap, safe query rather than an afternoon of
manually collating text files.

Three pressures make careful data management unavoidable:

- **Volume** — a university holds thousands of records, a bank holds
  billions; flat lists stop scaling long before that.
- **Concurrency** — two tellers, or two million app users, touch the
  same data at the same moment and must not corrupt it.
- **Durability** — a power cut mid-write must never leave balances
  half-updated or receipts lost.

## 2. Why Plain Files Weren't Enough

Before DBMSs, every application kept its own files and read them with
custom code. That worked until the files grew, and then the classic
problems appeared:

| Problem | What it looks like | The DBMS answer |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Redundancy | the same address copied into five departmental files | one normalised table, referenced by key |
| Inconsistency | copies disagree after one is updated | single source of truth plus constraints |
| Program-data dependence | renaming a column breaks every program reading the file | schema managed separately from programs |
| No concurrent access | two simultaneous writes corrupt the file | locks and transactions |
| Weak security | any program can read any byte | grants at table, row, and column level |
| No crash recovery | a half-written file after a power cut | logs, rollback, automatic recovery |
| Wasted effort | every app rewrites search and sort code | one optimised query engine for all |

[example]
A student changes her phone number. With five departmental files, five
programs must each find and rewrite her record — miss one, and the
exam cell calls an old number while the library calls the new one.
With a DBMS the number lives in one STUDENT row, and every system
reads the corrected value instantly.
[/example]

The same story, measured:

@chart bar title="Copies of one student's address that must be rewritten" labels:
Flat files (one per department)
Normalised DBMS

data:
5
1
@/chart

[warning]
Redundancy is not just wasted storage — it is a correctness risk. When
two copies disagree, at least one business decision is being made on
wrong data, and nobody knows which copy to trust.
[/warning]

## 3. The Relational Model: Tables All the Way Down

The dominant family of databases — and the one this subject mostly
studies — stores data as **relations**, which look like ordinary
tables. Each row is one record; each column is one property.

| Formal term | Everyday term | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Relation | table | STUDENT |
| Tuple | row | one student |
| Attribute | column | phone |
| Degree | number of columns | 4 |
| Cardinality | number of rows | 5,000 |
| Domain | allowed values of a column | 10-digit phone numbers |

A concrete STUDENT table:

| student_id | name | city | year |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | Aisha | Delhi | 2 |
| 2 | Boris | Mumbai | 1 |
| 3 | Chen | Delhi | 3 |

Two properties matter more than they first appear:

- **Schema versus instance** — the *schema* (table names, columns,
  types, constraints) is the blueprint and changes rarely; the
  *instance* (the current rows) changes every second. Courses teach
  schemas; production databases are instances.
- **No duplicate rows** — a relation is a *set* of tuples, so every
  row must be distinguishable by its values. That requirement is
  exactly what keys are for.

Creating the table in SQL:

```sql
CREATE TABLE student (
  student_id INT PRIMARY KEY,
  name       VARCHAR(50) NOT NULL,
  city       VARCHAR(30),
  year       SMALLINT CHECK (year BETWEEN 1 AND 4)
);

INSERT INTO student VALUES
  (1, 'Aisha', 'Delhi', 2),
  (2, 'Boris', 'Mumbai', 1),
  (3, 'Chen',  'Delhi', 3);
```

## 4. Keys: Naming and Linking Rows

Keys give every row a name and give tables a way to point at each
other — the relational model's disciplined substitute for pointers.

| Key | Meaning | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Superkey | any column set that uniquely identifies a row | student_id; also (student_id, name) |
| Candidate key | a *minimal* superkey — drop a column and uniqueness fails | student_id |
| Primary key | the candidate key chosen as the official identifier | student_id |
| Alternate key | a candidate key not chosen as primary | national ID number |
| Composite key | a key made of two or more columns together | (student_id, course_id) |
| Foreign key | a column referencing a key of another table | enrolment.student_id refers to student.student_id |

[definition]
A **foreign key** is a column (or set of columns) in one table whose
values must match a primary or unique key in another table. Enforcing
this — *referential integrity* — is what stops an enrolment from
pointing at a student who does not exist.
[/definition]

```sql
CREATE TABLE enrolment (
  student_id INT REFERENCES student(student_id),
  course_id  VARCHAR(8) REFERENCES course(course_id),
  grade      CHAR(2),
  PRIMARY KEY (student_id, course_id)   -- composite key
);
```

[tip]
Exam questions love the word *minimal* in candidate keys. (student_id,
name) uniquely identifies each row but is not minimal — student_id
alone already does — so it is only a superkey, never a candidate key.
[/tip]

## 5. Three Levels of Seeing, Two Kinds of Independence

The ANSI-SPARC architecture splits a database into three views so
different audiences never trip over each other:

| Level | Who sees it | What it holds |
| --- | --- | --- |
| External | each application or user group | a tailored view — the library sees names and fines, not fees |
| Conceptual | the designer | the whole logical schema: tables, keys, constraints |
| Internal | the engine | how bytes are stored: files, indexes, compression |

- **Physical data independence** — change storage structures (add an
  index, move to SSDs, repartition) without touching the conceptual
  schema. Every DBMS provides this well.
- **Logical data independence** — change the conceptual schema (add a
  column, split a table) without rewriting applications, by adjusting
  their external views. Much harder, and only partly achieved in
  practice.

[important]
Data independence is the single idea that separates a database from a
pile of files: programs describe *what* they want, never *where* or
*how* the data is stored. It is why renaming an index never requires
recompiling the accounts department.
[/important]

## 6. A Field Guide to Database Types

"Database" names a spectrum of engines, each optimised for a different
shape of data:

@diagram tree id="database-families" layout="tree" aria="Families of database systems"
  Databases -> "Relational (SQL)"
  Databases -> NoSQL
  Databases -> "Specialised engines"
  "Relational (SQL)" -> "MySQL, PostgreSQL, Oracle"
  NoSQL -> Document
  NoSQL -> Key-value
  NoSQL -> Column-family
  NoSQL -> Graph
  Document -> "MongoDB, CouchDB"
  Key-value -> "Redis, DynamoDB"
  Column-family -> "Cassandra, HBase"
  Graph -> "Neo4j, Neptune"
  "Specialised engines" -> "Time-series: InfluxDB"
  "Specialised engines" -> "Vector search: pgvector, FAISS"
@/diagram

| Family | Data shape | Strength | Typical use |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Relational | tables with fixed schemas | transactions, joins, integrity | banking, ERP, most business apps |
| Document | JSON-like blobs, flexible fields | rapid development, nested data | catalogues, content management |
| Key-value | opaque values addressed by key | extreme speed on simple lookup | caching, sessions, shopping carts |
| Column-family | rows grouped into column families | write-heavy scans at scale | IoT telemetry, analytics |
| Graph | nodes and edges | multi-hop relationship queries | social networks, fraud detection |
| Time-series | timestamped measurements | fast range scans, retention rules | monitoring, sensor feeds |

[info]
The pendulum swings both ways: NoSQL arose to escape rigid schemas and
the difficulty of scaling one machine, then relational engines adopted
JSON columns and distributed designs while document stores grew joins.
Modern practice is polyglot — choose per workload, not per fashion.
[/info]

## 7. Inside the DBMS: The Engine in Five Parts

A query such as `SELECT name FROM student WHERE city = 'Delhi'`
travels through the engine in stages:

@diagram flowchart id="query-journey" layout="hierarchical" aria="The path of a query through a DBMS"
node q label="SQL query arrives" shape="pill"
node p label="Parser checks syntax and names" shape="round-rect"
node o label="Optimiser chooses a plan (index scan or full scan)" shape="round-rect"
node e label="Executor fetches rows via the storage manager" shape="round-rect"
node t label="Transaction manager enforces ACID" shape="diamond"
node r label="Result returned; changes logged" shape="round-rect"

q -> p
p -> o
o -> e
e -> t
t -> r label="commit"
@/diagram

- **Query processor** — parses SQL, optimises it into a physical plan,
  and executes that plan. A good optimiser can beat a hand-tuned plan
  by orders of magnitude.
- **Storage manager** — files on disk, the **buffer pool** of pages
  cached in RAM, and the **indexes** (usually B-trees) that turn whole
  scans into a handful of seeks.
- **Transaction manager** — grants all-or-nothing behaviour and
  isolation between concurrent users.
- **Lock manager** — the transaction manager's enforcement arm.
- **Log manager** — durably records every change *before* applying it
  (write-ahead logging), so committed work survives a crash.

## 8. Who Uses a DBMS, and With What Language?

| Actor | Main tools | Concern |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Database administrator (DBA) | DDL, DCL, monitoring | availability, security, tuning |
| Schema designer | ER modelling, normalisation | a correct, stable schema |
| Application developer | DML inside programs | correct, fast queries |
| Analyst or end user | ad-hoc SELECT, reports | information, not plumbing |

The language they all share is SQL, whose sublanguages — DDL to
define, DML to manipulate, DCL to control access, TCL to manage
transactions — are the subject of the next lesson.

## 9. Quick Revision

| Concept | One-line recap |
| --- | --- |
| Database and DBMS | organised persistent data; software that manages it safely |
| File-system problems | redundancy, inconsistency, program-data dependence, no concurrency or recovery |
| Relation | table; tuple = row, attribute = column; no duplicate rows |
| Keys | superkey contains candidate keys; primary chosen; composite spans columns; foreign keys link tables |
| Three levels | external, conceptual, internal (ANSI-SPARC) |
| Independence | physical (storage) easy; logical (schema) harder |
| Families | relational for integrity; document, key-value, column, graph per workload |
| Engine parts | query processor, storage and buffer pool, transaction, lock, log managers |

[summary]
A database is organised, persistent, shared data; the DBMS is the
engine that makes searching, updating, and protecting it safe. The
relational model stores data in tables identified by keys — primary
keys name rows, foreign keys link tables — and hides storage details
behind three levels of abstraction. Under a DBMS, one normalised fact
replaces five inconsistent file copies, and every query flows through
parser, optimiser, executor, and transaction manager before returning.
[/summary]
